And I would say that that is better than half the power ups in this game, but not in terms

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:17.800
of raw power.

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:23.880
And something we see with power ups that we've seen in both Gradius and in, I think it was

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:25.800
Stinger? Is that those...

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.120
Twin B, right? I was thinking of a few, sorry.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:29.240
Yeah. Twin B is the Japanese.

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:30.180
Yeah. Right, right, yeah, yeah.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:35.560
Yeah. So, I think we saw in those games, definitely in Gradius, is that you stack power ups, you

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.540
know? Yeah. You can't do that in this one.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:42.300
And so, I wonder like, you know, obviously we're already talking about it's overpowered

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:46.080
just having the power ups alone, but which option do you prefer?

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:50.320
Do you prefer it when you only have like one big power up at a time, or do you prefer

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:54.700
to continue to like spec out your ship and have like this almighty powerful being?

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:56.920
I think it really kind of depends on how the game's designed.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:27:00.660
Yeah. Because in this game, if you were stacking the power ups, it would be not even a game.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:02.900
You'd be just watching your ship destroy everything.

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:05.820
Just because of the way, because the game's not designed for that, it doesn't seem like

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:07.500
it is. Because you can't do that, right?

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:08.420
You can't get more than one power up.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:13.800
No, yeah. I think that it depends on what you're going for with the game design, just like Joe said.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:20.020
It would, it's a lot more strategic to make it one at a time, so that you will try

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:22.560
and avoid other power ups if you don't want them.

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:26.680
Because yeah, now that you got a new one, that original one is gone.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:34.000
And I think if you're just going for the raw, like adrenaline rush, then yeah, you should

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:37.120
stack them. You should just make it a light show.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:43.100
Right. But one problem that this game doesn't have that an essential game like Gradius had was

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:47.000
that there was a point in Gradius where we all kind of admitted, if you die on level

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:52.280
six, right, and then you have to like play through level six back to the bare minimum ship,

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:55.500
you're in, you know, you're pretty much out of luck.

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:59.200
You know, like it's going to be really hard to collect enough stuff to get enough power

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:02.960
ups to get the ship back into a functioning order that's appropriate for level six.

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:08.660
In this game, no matter what level you're on, one or 12, you're still able to always access

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:12.840
power ups. I did kind of notice that the power ups didn't become as frequent in the later levels.

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:14.220
They give you a bunch of them in the beginning.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:15.440
But yeah, no, but yeah, exactly.

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:19.940
You still have the opportunity to always go back to like, oh, there's that tile six.

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:20.820
I really want that.

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:23.860
You know, I want to have the storm flash thing again.

00:28:24.300 --> 00:28:29.300
So you always have a chance to like, even if you die, once you start the level over, you

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:32.040
have a chance to like get your ship back up to the appropriate levels.

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:36.320
Right, right. And I will say the only other thing that like helps, I think, with the balance of the power

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:40.400
ups is the fact that sometimes it is a little challenging to get the power up you want,

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:46.080
because you don't have a ton of time once you see that like, yeah, you'll see like, oh,

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:50.040
there's the six, but I have like, but I'm already kind of hurtling towards it.

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:53.900
And you have to hit it a few times before you kind of release it and are able to grab

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:59.180
it. So like if I wasn't kind of lined up or ready to line up to start shooting it as soon as

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:01.620
I saw it, I might miss the opportunity to get it.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:30.660
Got it. And then the last thing we need, the last thing we need to talk about is the fortresses,

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:33.940
because they're, they're kind of the bosses in this game.

00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:38.140
Yeah. If you can call it that, it's just a, it basically like the screen stops scrolling, which was a

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:39.320
nice relief from my eyes.

00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:44.560
Yeah. And then you have this base or fortress depending on the level.

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:47.560
And it has like an eyeball in the middle of it.

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:49.360
I guess they look like eyeballs.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:52.840
It looks like an eyeball to me, but I couldn't tell for sure that the bases were sentient.

00:29:53.020 --> 00:29:54.240
You know, I just knew that they were.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:57.600
All of the stuff in this game just, they just look like stuff.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:29:59.700
Yeah. Like, like obstacles.

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:05.460
They somehow in some regards look like other ships, but most of the time they don't.

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:10.500
There were some creative solutions though, like two pieces of a ship that started at

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:13.180
the sides of the screen and come together to form one thing.

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:16.560
It was nice to see something different than just ships attacking you.

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:21.460
And specifically like things that might travel in different directions other than just,

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:24.600
you know, up to down or left to right.

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:26.100
Like there was a bunch of different options.

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:29.160
So I thought that was nice, but then those things show up plus the fortress.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.100
And I think you have like what?

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:32.540
60 seconds. It changes.

00:30:32.540 --> 00:30:34.780
It depends on which level you're on.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:39.200
And so you have like a limited amount of time where you should basically be focusing

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:43.960
on shooting the fortress, but you have to also take down all the enemies because otherwise

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:45.180
you're more likely going to die.

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:48.300
You don't have any access to power ups within this part.

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:49.780
If you didn't already collect them.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:55.760
Is this like a, is this a jarring thing considering how easy it was to get to that point?

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:57.700
And then all of a sudden the fortresses ramp up the difficulty?

00:30:57.940 --> 00:30:59.720
No, I think this is how it should like that.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:01.780
This was a good design choice.

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:06.920
You have your, you have your run up where you prepare for the most part.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:09.000
And yeah, this is where it counts.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:12.500
This is where whatever you better have the right power up that you want.

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:14.240
You had your opportunity.

00:31:14.740 --> 00:31:15.620
And I'll be honest.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:19.060
I didn't really know that the game was keeping track of whether or not I destroyed the fortress

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:20.660
until you said it a few minutes ago.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:24.960
And I kind of just figured out pretty early on that like you can just not destroy the fortress

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:31.200
and move on. And I just treated it as like survive for 60 seconds because destroying the fortress and

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:32.500
killing all the ships and avoiding them.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:33.580
I was like, that's too much for me.

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:34.800
I'm just going to try to avoid these ships.

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:36.320
You can do it a few times.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:37.640
Yeah, but I was just, yeah, I guess.

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:39.160
You're missing those sweet high score points.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:41.360
Right. But you know how, that's the thing I've always said.

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:42.740
I'm generally not a high score.

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:45.800
No, I know. No, no, no. We're self-admitted not high score people.

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:48.220
And that's just like, I think that that's normal, honestly.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.860
I think like if you didn't grow up in the arcade era, you probably, if you go back and

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:54.200
play these older games, you probably don't care for your high score.

00:31:54.240 --> 00:32:03.000
Sure. But if you go into a, if you go into an obstacle like the fortress in any game, wouldn't you

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:04.660
rather engage with the game?

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:08.780
No, you're right. Yeah, but I felt like I was engaging enough just by dodging and shooting the enemies.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:12.340
I was like, I guess I'm supposed to be killing this thing, but it's the game, at least to

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:15.600
my perspective, the game wasn't punishing me for not killing the fortress.

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:17.300
So I was like, I'll just keep killing the ships.

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:19.440
I see. Okay. And I thought that was almost like an option.

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:22.900
Like there was, I didn't, I didn't understand my incentive to kill the fortress.

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:28.660
From Joe's point of view, if you go, if the whole goal is to get to the system and destroy

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:32.060
that thing, it'll just blow up the fortress itself after you blow up the system.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:34.120
So why even bother with those things right now?

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:38.680
Just make sure you survive. And I did just assume you would get bonus points or something for destroying the fortress.

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:42.240
I assumed it was like an optional like extra bonus thing, which I was just like, I just

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:43.680
want to see where this game goes at this point.

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:45.860
And little did you know you were making it harder.

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:46.880
Yes, I guess I was.

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:50.880
Also, don't forget Sean, that there are crazy people that like play through the Dark Souls

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:55.100
games and just run past every single enemy except for the mandatory boss fights.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:56.400
Yeah. No, I know that.

00:32:56.540 --> 00:32:58.260
I'm just saying like, I don't agree with that.

00:32:58.260 --> 00:33:01.740
I think that they're robbing themselves of an experience, but there are definitely people

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:02.740
that like decide.

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:05.600
There are also speed runners. They've probably played the game the correct way before.

00:33:05.900 --> 00:33:08.320
Oh, no, no, no. I'm not saying for speed runners.

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:13.100
I'm saying there are also like real players who have decided like, it'd be easier for me

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:15.420
to go around these guys than face them.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:19.300
True. And I will say, I don't know neither of you are actually saying this, but I don't think

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:24.820
me not fighting these fortresses is making me miss out on the same amount of things that

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:25.720
I would miss out on. No, it's not.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:27.100
I know you're not saying that.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:28.580
It's just a stain on your character.

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:29.860
Sure. Of course. Yes.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:30.920
I'm a horrible guy.

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:34.380
I actually have to wonder what else you opt out of in life if you choose to opt out

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:35.280
of a fortress battle.

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:39.400
So you're saying that when you sign up to a website, you opt out of their newsletter?

00:33:40.700 --> 00:33:41.720
No, newsletters all day.

00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:43.480
I read. I'm just making sure you're not a monster.

00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:46.220
That's why I couldn't didn't have time to fight the fortress because I had all these

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:49.160
newsletters to read from from websites I signed up for.

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:52.580
And I'll just point out like random nostalgia fact.

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:54.500
There are like four people signed up for a newsletter.

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:58.780
We don't have one. I'm so sorry to those people.

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:02.900
I never got around to writing one, but there is a sign up form for the newsletter.

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:05.480
So we never sold your emails.

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:07.500
Our annual newsletter. Our yearly newsletter.

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:11.240
Well, then I think we should go into development history.

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:16.080
And usually that's not a very exciting thing as of late, but I think that there's some I

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:17.480
think there's some good stuff in here.

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:21.740
Okay. So let me just hit you with the facts and then you guys react.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:23.260
Okay. Facts react.

00:34:23.660 --> 00:34:24.580
That's what we're calling this segment.

00:34:25.240 --> 00:34:34.000
All right. So Zanuck was designed by compile and published in Japan by Pony Canyon and in North America

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:38.240
by FCI. Now, if those names sound familiar, compile and FCI.

00:34:38.240 --> 00:34:41.260
That was who we talked about in the lunar pool episode.

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:42.820
Those are the Puyo Puyo guys.

00:34:43.140 --> 00:34:44.700
You love this so much.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:48.860
I mean, like we almost did a whole episode of Puyo Puyo in that lunar pool thing.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:50.320
I'm not here to talk about that again.

00:34:50.340 --> 00:34:53.920
I just wanted to remind everybody that the guys who made lunar pool essentially also made

00:34:53.920 --> 00:34:55.460
Zanuck. And Puyo Puyo.

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:56.880
Puyo Puyo.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:57.900
Yeah. Puyo Puyo.

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:01.560
And then Pony Canyon.

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:03.700
Great name. Right.

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:05.520
Great name. Sounds awesome.

00:35:05.800 --> 00:35:06.740
Just a cool name.

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:10.360
Unfortunately. They don't really publish many video games.

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:11.880
What do they do usually?

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:16.800
They usually publish like music and movies in Japan and stuff like that.

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:18.140
So all publishing stuff.

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:23.080
But when I looked at their video game stuff, it was mostly just like Famicom and MSX games

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:24.600
that they were responsible for publishing.

00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:29.080
So it would make sense that they were doing it around this time, but they didn't really

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:33.240
publish anything too noticeable other than like, I think they also published Pitfall, but

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:36.400
just in Japan, not in North America.

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:38.880
So that's just like a weird anomaly thing.

00:35:39.200 --> 00:35:42.820
Zanuck was initially released in 1986 for the MSX computer.

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:46.700
We've talked about the MSX like a lot just by calling it the MSX, but you guys know what

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:53.140
that is? I only know that the MSX, the MSX 2 had the original Metal Gear on it.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:54.420
Yes, that's true.

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:55.960
That's a good trivia stat.

00:35:56.200 --> 00:36:02.500
Yeah. The MSX essentially like, it's one of those like Commodore 64 like computers that was more

00:36:02.500 --> 00:36:05.340
known for video gaming than it was ever known for home computing.

00:36:05.620 --> 00:36:10.720
You know? So I think like we've been saying MSX a lot, but I just want to say that it is a very important

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:12.800
console to like the Japanese market.

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:16.060
Even if it's not a console per se, it's a home computer.

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:19.960
It was also like bigger in Europe, right?

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:25.780
It was never really... I think the MSX was bigger in Europe, but also like, that's like saying that the Master

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:27.480
System was bigger in Europe.

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:31.780
Like the Master System didn't sell well here in North America, but it didn't sell like hot

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:32.620
cakes in Europe. I see.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:33.580
It just sold better.

00:36:33.860 --> 00:36:35.300
So like people knew more about it.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:42.280
And then the creator of Zanac or rather the games designer, it was a team creation, but

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:48.020
Masamitsu Nitani, who goes by Moo, that's his nickname, M-O-O.

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:52.780
He's the game designer for Zanac, but he's also the company president.

00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:57.420
Huh. I think that's pretty cool when you're the president of your company is also like, but I'm going

00:36:57.420 --> 00:36:58.480
to design the game.

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:05.120
Very cool. Because we've seen that before with like Sakaguchi, who some people think is the president of

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:10.180
Square, but is really just like getting the orders from his boss to like make 3D games.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:12.300
That sounds good. Like we want 3D.

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:17.160
So this is a guy who actually says like, I wanted to make this game, so I did.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:18.960
And we as a company will make it.

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:24.060
It's a little intimidating that other people have to program the game that your president

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:25.040
has designed. Yeah.

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:28.080
But hey, I say when it's Compile, right?

00:37:28.280 --> 00:37:30.840
Like it's a smaller, it's not like a Nintendo thing.

00:37:31.060 --> 00:37:32.280
Yeah. So I'm okay with this.

00:37:32.360 --> 00:37:33.480
Four guys in a basement. Yeah.

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:34.960
Maybe. Essentially. Maybe.

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:41.780
But he was interviewed way back in like 97, I feel like this interview was from.

00:37:41.980 --> 00:37:47.940
Oh, I can't wait. And he was interviewed mostly about like some MSX stuff and just the history of like Compile's

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:51.700
beginnings. So here's the interview for everybody to hear.

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:53.480
This is the interviewer.

00:37:53.880 --> 00:37:58.300
Speaking of games ported from the MSX, Zanuck was ported to the Famicom.

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:00.480
Nitani responds with,

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:05.800
The MSX version of Zanuck was published by Pony Canyon, but it was the Famicom version

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:07.360
that got really great reviews.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:13.060
It didn't sell a ton of copies, but it raised Compile's brand recognition a lot.

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:18.000
I heard that people in the arcade world thought very well of it too, which made me happy.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:19.600
The interviewer asks,

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:22.380
How did you get started making Famicom games?

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:23.800
Nitani responds,

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:30.120
Pony Canyon, who had seen EI, another game that they made, asked us to port Zanuck.

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.460
It took us two years to make.

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:34.300
We're slow. Laughs.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:40.180
After that, we created the billiards game, Lunar Pool, which featured unique pool tables.

00:38:40.400 --> 00:38:44.420
I don't recall us ever making much money on our Famicom games, unfortunately.

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:45.760
He doesn't remember though.

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:51.420
He's not sure. Yeah, the company president can't exactly recall the figures of the Famicom sales.

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:58.780
But I think it's interesting to think that like, here's a guy who was working during the early days of the NES and the Famicom,

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:03.780
making games directly responsible for all aspects of his company.

00:39:04.300 --> 00:39:07.460
And he's just kind of like, yeah, we made this game.

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:10.220
I don't really know if it made money, but people liked it.

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:11.340
So I'm cool with that.

00:39:11.620 --> 00:39:14.360
You know, like you don't really hear people say that about like failure.

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:17.820
Like he had another job where he's making all his money and this was just like a zombie.

00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:18.880
He's actually like a doctor. Yeah.

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:20.980
Dr. Moo. Right.

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:22.620
If they're going after his nickname.

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:27.780
Oh, yeah. And so I thought that that was a sub, you know, like a...

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:31.760
Like a thumbs up. Yeah, thumbs up interview in terms of the interviews that we usually get.

00:39:31.820 --> 00:39:32.900
Yeah, it was cogent.

00:39:33.080 --> 00:39:34.180
Yeah, which is good.

00:39:34.340 --> 00:39:35.840
We need more cogent interviews.

